```tsx
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import { DndContext, closestCenter } from '@dnd-kit/core';
import { arrayMove, SortableContext, verticalListSortingStrategy } from '@dnd-kit/sortable';
import { db } from './firebaseConfig'; // Your Firebase config
import { doc, onSnapshot, updateDoc } from 'firebase/firestore';

const Board = () => {
  const [tasks, setTasks] = useState([]);

  // Real-time synchronization with Firebase
  useEffect(() => {
    const unsub = onSnapshot(doc(db, "boards", "main-board"), (doc) => {
      setTasks(doc.data().tasks);
    });
    return () => unsub();
  }, []);

  // Handle Drag End
  const handleDragEnd = async (event) => {
    const { active, over } = event;
    if (active.id !== over.id) {
      const oldIndex = tasks.findIndex(t => t.id === active.id);
      const newIndex = tasks.findIndex(t => t.id === over.id);
      const newTasks = arrayMove(tasks, oldIndex, newIndex);

      setTasks(newTasks);
      // Persist to Firebase for real-time collaboration
      await updateDoc(doc(db, "boards", "main-board"), { tasks: newTasks });
    }
  };

  return (
    <DndContext collisionDetection={closestCenter} onDragEnd={handleDragEnd}>
      <SortableContext items={tasks} strategy={verticalListSortingStrategy}>
        <div className="board">
          {tasks.map(task => <TaskItem key={task.id} id={task.id} title={task.title} />)}
        </div>
      </SortableContext>
    </DndContext>
  );
};
```

### Key Components of this approach:

1.  **`<DndContext>`**: This provides the wrapper that tracks mouse/touch movement and handles the drag-and-drop state.
2.  **`onSnapshot` (Firebase)**: This is the secret sauce for **real-time collaboration**. It sets up a persistent WebSocket connection to your database. Whenever any user modifies the document, your application state updates automatically without refreshing.
3.  **`arrayMove`**: A utility from `dnd-kit` that calculates the new position of your array after a move, ensuring your UI list matches your data structure.
4.  **`SortableContext`**: Maps your data IDs to the DOM elements, allowing the library to calculate layout shifts during the drag interaction.

### How to scale this for production:
*   **Optimistic Updates**: In the `handleDragEnd`, you are currently updating the UI immediately (`setTasks`) while the database call happens in the background. This makes the app feel snappy even on slow networks.
*   **Permissions**: Use Firebase Security Rules to ensure that only authenticated users can edit specific project boards.
*   **State Management**: As your application grows (adding columns, labels, assignments), you may want to integrate **Zustand** or **Redux Toolkit** to manage the global state of your board rather than keeping it local to the component.
*   **Backend**: If you move away from Firebase, you would replace `updateDoc` with a `fetch` request to a Node.js/Go backend using **WebSockets (Socket.io)** to broadcast the drag event to other connected clients.

