We will use a circular buffer (a Single-Producer, Single-Consumer queue) for inter-thread communication, ensuring that the thread receiving market data is never blocked by the thread processing the order logic.

#### 1. Core Data Structure: `RingBuffer.hpp`
This structure allows the Network Thread to drop incoming market updates into a queue that the Strategy Thread consumes without using `std::mutex`.

```cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <vector>

template<typename T, size_t Size>
class RingBuffer {
private:
    T buffer[Size];
    std::atomic<size_t> head{0};
    std::atomic<size_t> tail{0};

public:
    bool push(const T& item) {
        size_t t = tail.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
        size_t next_t = (t + 1) % Size;
        if (next_t == head.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) return false; // Full
        buffer[t] = item;
        tail.store(next_t, std::memory_order_release);
        return true;
    }

    bool pop(T& item) {
        size_t h = head.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
        if (h == tail.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) return false; // Empty
        item = buffer[h];
        head.store((h + 1) % Size, std::memory_order_release);
        return true;
    }
};
```

#### 2. The Execution Engine: `main.cpp`
This represents the separation of concerns: one thread handles network/input, the other handles the business logic.

