This implementation follows a "pull" pattern: it monitors a file size and rotates it (renaming the old file and creating a new one) if it exceeds a specified limit, keeping only a fixed number of backups.

### Rust Implementation

```rust
use std::fs;
use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};
use std::thread;
use std::time::Duration;

const LOG_FILE: &str = "/var/log/myapp.log";
const MAX_SIZE_BYTES: u64 = 10 * 1024 * 1024; // 10MB
const MAX_BACKUPS: u32 = 3;
const CHECK_INTERVAL: Duration = Duration::from_secs(30);

fn rotate_logs(path: &str) -> std::io::Result<()> {
    // Shift existing backups (e.g., .log.2 -> .log.3)
    for i in (1..MAX_BACKUPS).rev() {
        let old = format!("{}.{}", path, i);
        let new = format!("{}.{}", path, i + 1);
        if Path::new(&old).exists() {
            fs::rename(old, new)?;
        }
    }
    // Rename current log to .1
    fs::rename(path, format!("{}.1", path))?;
    // Create new empty log
    fs::File::create(path)?;
    Ok(())
}

fn main() {
    let log_path = PathBuf::from(LOG_FILE);

    loop {
        if let Ok(metadata) = fs::metadata(&log_path) {
            if metadata.len() >= MAX_SIZE_BYTES {
                if let Err(e) = rotate_logs(LOG_FILE) {
                    eprintln!("Failed to rotate log: {}", e);
                }
            }
        }

        // Sleep to keep CPU and memory usage at near-zero between checks
        thread::sleep(CHECK_INTERVAL);
    }
}
```

### Why this is memory-efficient:
1.  **No Heap Fragmentation:** The logic avoids complex data structures. It uses stack-allocated variables and simple file system calls.
