### The Project: Secure Enterprise API
This snippet shows how to configure a service that authenticates via Azure Active Directory (Microsoft Entra ID) and accesses Azure resources securely.

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer;
using Microsoft.Identity.Web;
using Azure.Identity;
using Azure.Security.KeyVault.Secrets;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// 1. Setup Authentication: Integrate with Microsoft Entra ID (Azure AD)
builder.Services.AddAuthentication(JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme)
    .AddMicrosoftIdentityWebApi(builder.Configuration.GetSection("AzureAd"));

builder.Services.AddControllers();

// 2. Setup Azure Managed Identity
// This allows your service to access Azure resources (Key Vault, SQL, etc.)
// without managing sensitive credentials in your code/config files.
builder.Services.AddSingleton(new SecretClient(
    new Uri("https://your-vault-name.vault.azure.net/"), 
    new DefaultAzureCredential()));

var app = builder.Build();

app.UseAuthentication();
app.UseAuthorization();

// 3. Define a Protected Enterprise Endpoint
app.MapGet("/api/data", async (SecretClient secretClient) =>
{
    // Fetch a secret securely from Azure Key Vault at runtime
    KeyVaultSecret secret = await secretClient.GetSecretAsync("DbConnectionString");

    return Results.Ok(new { message = "Data retrieved securely using Managed Identity." });
})
.RequireAuthorization(); // Requires a valid Entra ID token

app.Run();
```

### Why this is the "Enterprise" approach:

1.  **`DefaultAzureCredential`**: This is the gold standard for Azure development. It automatically detects the identity of the environment. When you run this locally, it uses your Visual Studio/Azure CLI login. When you deploy it to an **Azure App Service** or **AKS**, it automatically uses the resource's assigned Managed Identity. No secrets are stored in your code.
2.  **`AddMicrosoftIdentityWebApi`**: This integrates natively with Microsoft Entra ID. It ensures that your backend service only accepts requests from authenticated users or applications within your corporate directory.
3.  **ASP.NET Core Performance**: This code runs on the Kestrel web server, which is highly optimized for Windows and Linux containers, matching the performance profiles required by large-scale enterprise services.

